<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bedia Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/999">999</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bedia Cathedral is a medieval church in eastern Abkhazia, founded in 999 by Bagrat III, king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abkhaz-people">Abkhazians</a> and the first king of a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, who was buried there in 1014. Its gold Bedia Chalice, a gift of the king, is one of the masterpieces of Georgian metalwork. The church was a seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> of western Georgia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bedia-cathedral.jpg" alt="Bedia Cathedral" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Inalo1989 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bedia2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The church was built at the close of the 10th century and consecrated in 999 at the wish of King Bagrat II of Abkhazia, who went on to rule a united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> as Bagrat III. According to the Georgian chronicler, he made it the seat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. He and his mother, Queen Gurandukht, are buried there. Within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicate-of-abkhazia">Catholicate of Abkhazia</a>, Bedia was the centre of a diocese, and over the centuries the see was an important ecclesiastical, cultural and educational centre. Services ceased in the 17th century and resumed in the second half of the 19th.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>The complex stands in Bedia, in the Tkvarcheli district of Abkhazia, a disputed region on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> coast. Its main building, the church of the Virgin, stands in the middle of a walled courtyard and was much rebuilt between the 13th and 15th centuries. It is a cross-in-square church built of cleanly trimmed sandstone, with a dome of 14 facets and as many windows resting on two free-standing pillars in the west and on the apses in the east. A bell tower of the 12th to 14th centuries rises over the gateway, and to the west lie the ruins of a two-storeyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' palace.</p><p>Three layers of murals survive inside, dating from the 10th and 11th, the 13th and 14th, and the 16th and 17th centuries; those of the southern wall include portraits of the king and of members of the Dadiani family. Inscriptions in the Georgian Asomtavruli script record the church's building and repairs.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the 16th century Archbishop Anton Zhuanisdze built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>' palace and set up a rich library, where old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> were restored and theological works translated. By the end of the 19th century the church stood abandoned and overgrown, though most of the dome survived; early in the 20th century Russian monks and nuns, intending to renovate it, removed the damaged dome entirely. The palace's floors were repaired in the restoration of 1968-69.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bedia Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Churches such as Mokvi and <b>Bedia</b> show its wealth, and the kingdom controlled the Black Sea coast and Rioni valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-abkhazia">Kingdom of Abkhazia</a></li></ul></section>
